select smttime, 
TRY_CAST(
        CASE 
            WHEN smttime IS NULL THEN NULL
            ELSE 
                -- 1. Convert float to integer, then to a string
                -- 2. Right-pad it out with leading zeros so it's always 4 characters (e.g., '930' becomes '0930')
                -- 3. Insert the ':' between hours and minutes
                STUFF(RIGHT('0000' + CAST(CAST(smttime AS INT) AS VARCHAR(4)), 4), 3, 0, ':')
        END AS TIME(0)
    ) AS smttime
    from Bronze.Himalayan_Exped; 

## Explanation of the above
CAST(smttime AS INT)
What it does: It strips off the decimal point by forcing the float value into a whole integer.
Example: 1530.0 becomes 1530. 900.0 becomes 900.

Step B: Changing Numbers to Text SQL
CAST(... AS VARCHAR(4))

What it does: It converts that raw integer into a string of text characters so that SQL Server can manipulate it like words.
Example: The number 1530 becomes the string text '1530'.

Step C: Padding Morning Times with Zeros
RIGHT('0000' + ..., 4)
What it does: If an expedition reached the summit at 9:00 AM, the text is only 3 characters long ('900'). 
This step glues 0000 to the front of the text ('0000900'), and then chops off everything except the rightmost 4 characters.
Example:
'1530' stays '1530'
'900' becomes '0900' (safely creating a standardized 4-character time string!).

Step D: Injecting the Colon (:)
SQL
STUFF(..., 3, 0, ':')
What it does: The STUFF function deletes and inserts characters. 
The arguments mean: "Go to position 3, delete 0 characters, and insert a ':' there."
Example:
'1530' becomes '15:30'
'0900' becomes '09:00'

Guardrails & Data Type Casting
Now that the string looks like a standard time format, the outer functions make sure it integrates cleanly into your database without crashing:

TRY_CAST(
    CASE 
        WHEN smttime IS NULL THEN NULL
        ELSE ...
    END AS TIME(0)
) AS smttime


/* Before performing any text manipulation, the code checks WHEN smttime IS NULL THEN NULL.  
If a row has a missing summit time, it passes right through as a native database NULL instead of processing it, preventing errors.

Why use TIME(0)? The (0) means "zero decimal places for seconds". 
It keeps your time tight and clean as HH:MM:SS (e.g., 15:30:00) without trailing fractional milliseconds.
Why use TRY_CAST instead of regular CAST? 
In large datasets, there is always a chance of corrupt data (for example, a typo in the source file like 2560.0).

A normal CAST function would crash your entire migration script.
TRY_CAST is a defensive programming function: if it runs into an impossible time,
it will gracefully turn that single row into a NULL and allow your query to finish successfully.*/

