 /* EXPLANATION OF THE ABOVE 

 Layer 1 — The Innermost Piece: Extracting the Primary Nation
This is the core extraction logic. It appears twice in the expression — once in the CASE input and once in the ELSE. Understanding it once means you understand both.
sqlTRIM(LEFT(
    TRIM(nation),
    CHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/') - 1
))
There are four functions here, working from the inside out:

TRIM(nation)
sqlTRIM(nation)
Simply removes any leading or trailing spaces from the raw nation value before anything else happens. This prevents bugs where ' UK' or 'Nepal ' would fail to match correctly.
So '  Nepal/Australia  ' becomes 'Nepal/Australia'.

TRIM(nation) + '/'
sqlTRIM(nation) + '/'
Appends a / to the end of the string.
So 'Nepal/Australia' becomes 'Nepal/Australia/'
And 'France' becomes 'France/'
Why? Because of what comes next — CHARINDEX needs to find a /. For a single-country value like 'France' there is no / in the original string, so CHARINDEX would return 0, which would break the LEFT calculation. By adding a / at the end, you guarantee there is always at least one slash to find, even for single-country values.

CHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/')
sqlCHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/')
CHARINDEX finds the position of the first occurrence of a character inside a string. It returns a number.

CHARINDEX(...) - 1
sqlCHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/') - 1
Subtracts 1 from the position. This gives you the length of the primary nation name — everything before the first slash.

Examples:
nationCHARINDEXminus 1'Nepal/Australia'65 ? length of "Nepal"'France'76 ? length of "France"'USA/UK/Japan'43 ? length of "USA"

LEFT(TRIM(nation), ...)
sqlLEFT(TRIM(nation), CHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/') - 1)
LEFT takes the first N characters of a string. Here N is the number you just calculated — the length of the primary nation name.
Examples:
nationNLEFT result'Nepal/Australia'5'Nepal''France'6'France''USA/UK/Japan'3'USA'

The outer TRIM
sqlTRIM(LEFT(...))
One final trim around the result in case there were spaces around the slash, e.g. 'Nepal / Australia' would give 'Nepal ' from LEFT, so the outer TRIM cleans that trailing space off.

What you have now
After all of this, you have extracted a single clean value — the primary nation — from whatever raw string was in the column:
'Nepal/Australia/UK'  ?  'Nepal'
'France'              ?  'France'
'USA/UK/Japan'        ?  'USA'
'UK'                  ?  'UK'

Layer 2 — The CASE: Standardising the Extracted Value
Now that you have a clean single nation name, the CASE expression checks it and replaces non-standard variants with their standard form.
sqlCASE  TRIM(LEFT(TRIM(nation), CHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/') - 1))
    WHEN 'UK'            THEN 'United Kingdom'
    WHEN 'England'       THEN 'United Kingdom'
    WHEN 'Great Britain' THEN 'United Kingdom'
    ...
    WHEN 'USA'           THEN 'United States'
    WHEN 'US'            THEN 'United States'
    ...
    ELSE TRIM(LEFT(TRIM(nation), CHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/') - 1))
END
This is a simple CASE expression. SQL Server evaluates the expression once, then walks down the WHEN list until it finds a match.

If the extracted value is 'UK' ? it returns 'United Kingdom'
If the extracted value is 'USA' ? it returns 'United States'
If the extracted value is 'Nepal' ? no WHEN matches ? falls to ELSE

The ELSE clause:
sqlELSE TRIM(LEFT(
        TRIM(nation),
        CHARINDEX('/', TRIM(nation) + '/') - 1
     ))
This repeats the same extraction logic. It means: if the value does not need standardising, just return the extracted value as-is.
So 'Nepal', 'France', 'Japan', 'India' — anything already clean — passes straight through unchanged.*/

